\section{Index Values by Contexts, Not One Hidden Counter}

Intensional Lucid generalizes a stream index to a context: a finite mapping from named dimensions to tags. An expression denotes a value as a function of context. The indexical \(\#d\) observes the current tag in dimension \(d\); context switching evaluates an expression at a modified context. Ordinary one-dimensional time is the special case with one natural-number dimension.

Dimension names matter. A compiler-introduced recursion dimension must be fresh so that shifting it does not shift the source's time dimension. Conversely, a variable independent of a dimension can share a cached value across tags in that dimension, but only after a valid dimensional-dependence analysis.

\section{Sample a Two-Dimensional Definition}

In common indexical Lucid notation, \texttt{\#} queries a dimension and \texttt{@} changes it:

\begin{verbatim}
dimension time, sensor;
reading = input @ [sensor : #sensor, time : #time];
previous = if #time == 0 then 0
           else reading @ [time : #time - 1];
delta = reading - previous;
\end{verbatim}

At context \([\texttt{time}:4,\texttt{sensor}:2]\), \texttt{previous} reads the same sensor at time \(3\). The context override changes only \texttt{time}; silently resetting \texttt{sensor} would select another value. \Cref{fig:visual-359} shows the context as coordinates rather than as an execution timestamp.

