\section{Evaluate an Override Precisely}

\begin{verbatim}
eval(E @ [d : T], context c):
    tag := eval(T, c)
    c2 := copy(c)
    c2[d] := tag
    return eval(E, c2)

eval(#d, context c):
    return c[d]
\end{verbatim}

The tag expression is evaluated in the original context \(c\); then \(E\) is evaluated in the overridden context \(c_2\). This ordering must be defined rather than inferred from an implementation's mutable context object.

\section{Compile Context Dependence Explicitly}

The IR should include expression and binding nodes, dependency edges labeled with context transformations, dimension declarations and tag types, explicit delay/state only when a sequential target is chosen, logical clocks for dimensions that have clock interpretations, effects and partiality, and provenance for every indexical and introduced dimension. Compilation may produce demand code keyed by projected contexts, tagged-dataflow tasks, or loops nested over finite requested regions.

Legal optimizations include dropping a cache-key dimension proven irrelevant and composing adjacent context overrides. Semantic-preservation risks arise if an indexical, effect, or called function observes the removed dimension. Flattening two dimensions to one integer can overflow or alter ordering unless the mapping is injective over the demanded region. Preserve values per expression and context, not merely the sequence in which a particular evaluator happened to request them.

\section{Project a Context Only After Inspecting Its Uses}

Add a third dimension, \texttt{mode}, which the \texttt{delta} definition never queries. Let sensor A report \(7,11\), and let sensor B report \(20,23\), at times zero and one. If the input primitive depends only on the two explicit tags, the warehouse can share \texttt{delta} between modes but not between sensors. This is a property of the expression and primitive contracts, not a general privilege to remove the last coordinate of a context.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lrrr}
\hline
Demand & Reading & Previous & Delta \\
\hline
A at time 0, raw & 7 & 0 & 7 \\
A at time 1, raw & 11 & 7 & 4 \\
A at time 1, display & 11 & 7 & 4 \\
B at time 1, display & 23 & 20 & 3 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Analyze one-dimensional overrides structurally. For a constant tag, an override can remove a dependency on the original coordinate. For \texttt{time := \#time - 1}, however, the replacement expression needs the original time. With multiple overrides, first specify whether their right-hand sides share the original context or see earlier updates; the following rule applies to a single override.

\begin{verbatim}
dimensions(node):
    match node:
        Constant(k): return {}
        Indexical(d): return {d}
        Override(body, d, tag):
            return (dimensions(body) - {d}) union dimensions(tag)
        Primitive(op, operands):
            return declared_context_reads(op) union
                   union(dimensions(x) for x in operands)
        Conditional(test, yes, no):
            return dimensions(test) union dimensions(yes)
                   union dimensions(no)
\end{verbatim}

Propagate dependencies through named definitions to a fixed point. If an opaque primitive has no declared read set, assume that it can inspect the whole context. The union at conditionals overapproximates what an individual demand needs, which is safe for caching but can miss some sharing. Test projection by repeating the two A demands at time one, then making the B demand without flushing the warehouse. An implementation that accidentally projects away \texttt{sensor} returns \(4\) for B, which the independently chosen fixture exposes. Repeating the test in reverse order catches the same error even when the first lookup always succeeds.

\paragraph{Check the key at the call boundary.}
When the tag is a constant, compare two caller contexts that differ only
in the overridden coordinate. The body should be evaluated in the same
callee context. When the tag is an indexical expression, instead check
that it is evaluated in the caller context before the key is projected.
